; the fourth state of matter. ionised gas: electrons stripped from atoms.
; temperature so high that thermal energy exceeds ionisation energy.
; free electrons and positive ions move independently.
; electrically conductive — responds strongly to electromagnetic fields.
; most visible matter in the universe is plasma: stars, nebulae, lightning.
; 
; examples:
; stars: hydrogen plasma at 10⁷ K at core. nuclear fusion occurs here.
; lightning: air briefly ionised at ~30,000 K.
; aurora: solar wind plasma interacts with earth's magnetic field.
; neon signs: low-pressure plasma in noble gas discharge tubes.
; fusion reactor: plasma confined by magnetic field (tokamak, stellarator).
; 
; plasma physics: governed by magnetohydrodynamics (MHD).
; Debye length: distance over which charge is screened in plasma.
; plasma frequency: characteristic oscillation of free electrons.
; 
; distinct from gas: plasma responds to EM fields (conducting).
; distinct from solid/liquid: no fixed structure.
; the phase transition from gas to plasma is ionisation — not a sharp boundary.
